lesson physics-a-circuit-that-works v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A circuit that works"
  goal "Say whether a bulb, a buzzer or a motor will work, by following the wire all the way round."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      row two at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        circuit shut closed=1 cells=1
        circuit open closed=0 cells=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Electricity only flows round a loop that is joined all the way. The cell gives the push, the
      wire carries it, and the switch is a gap you can open and close. One gap anywhere and nothing
      happens.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.switch-state count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.does-it-light count=2 seed=4
    practice physics.buzzer-sounds count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.motor-turns count=2 seed=3
    show physics.circuit-parts
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.which-dark w=0 f=1 c=2 hard-f=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.buzzer-and-bulb c=2 easy-c=1 hard-c=3
  }
  remember {
    say "One gap in the loop and the bulb stays dark."
  }

  grown-ups """
    If there is a bulb, a battery and two wires in the house, build it. The moment worth catching is
    lifting one wire off: the bulb goes out at once, which is what "a complete circuit" means. The
    last question hides the bulb and says only that the buzzer is sounding; the answer follows from
    the loop being whole, which is the idea the lesson rests on.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the faults are open switches, which show at a glance."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the faults are loose wires, which are found only by following the loop round."
  }
}
